Dialogue: 0,0:43:29.99,0:43:36.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,And another one I will talk about today is the configuration manifold that describes Lagrangian and Hamiltonian dynamics.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:37.04,0:43:42.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,So all the mathematical spaces that you just saw here are all smooth manifolds or differentiable manifolds.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:42.26,0:43:46.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,They are essentially curved space that locally look but globally are not.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:46.43,0:43:54.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the idea here is to use all their geometries as inductive biases to guide the robot learning algorithm and achieve higher performance,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:54.65,0:44:00.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,enhance data efficiency and also to endo them with some additional guarantees.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:01.04,0:44:06.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me start with a very simple example that illustrates why considering geometry is actually relevant.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:06.68,0:44:11.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can think here about the sphere manifold that we use to encode orientation as a unit quaternion.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:11.95,0:44:15.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we simply want to compute the mean of three points on the sphere.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:15.55,0:44:17.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can do that in a classical Euclidean way.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:17.91,0:44:21.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,You take them, you average them and you actually obtain something that is inside the sphere.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:21.96,0:44:24.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it doesn't give you an orientation that is valid.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:24.09,0:44:27.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,So typically what we do is some kind of post-processing step.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:27.28,0:44:29.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,But this can actually lead to some issues.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:29.84,0:44:31.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,Another way is to take spherical coordinates instead.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:32.02,0:44:34.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you are sure that the mean will lie on the sphere.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:34.80,0:44:39.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in that case the mean actually is completely out of the support of the data distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:39.33,0:44:44.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what you need to do is to actually consider the geometry of the sphere and comput a geometric mean which
Dialogue: 0,0:44:44.67,0:44:51.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,will be completely independent of the coordinates that you choose and that will give you the real mean of those three data.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:51.92,0:44:56.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I hope that you're convinced that with such a simple example we already have issues if we don't consider geometry.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:56.61,0:45:00.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's actually important for all types of robot learning algorithms.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:00.16,0:45:06.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in the remaining of the talk I will show you example of applications where we use the inductive bias that arises from geometry which
Dialogue: 0,0:45:06.19,0:45:10.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,is sometimes complemented with other types of inductive biases from physics or from
Dialogue: 0,0:45:10.08,0:45:15.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,control theory for example um to see how we can enhance robot learning.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:15.17,0:45:22.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me start with an example where we learn motion policies that encode both position and orientation.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:22.40,0:45:26.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we use flow matching which I guess all of you know is a powerful generative model for
Dialogue: 0,0:45:26.53,0:45:34.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,imitation learning due to its ability to handle highdimensional multimodal data distribution um that are conditioned um on vision data.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:34.62,0:45:39.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here what we do is that we take an action from a simple base distribution
Dialogue: 0,0:45:39.12,0:45:44.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,and typically we flow it until a more complex target distribution and flow matching does that
Dialogue: 0,0:45:44.08,0:45:47.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,by simply defining a vector field a desired vector field that defines the flow and by
Dialogue: 0,0:45:47.90,0:45:54.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,simply learning a neural network that would be conditioned on your observation um to predict the flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:54.40,0:45:58.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and then when you want to do inference you simply use integration of your vector field.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:58.36,0:46:02.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,you obtain your flow and then you can have such kind of robot motion policies where
Dialogue: 0,0:46:02.27,0:46:07.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,here we learned we did we we taught a robot to move a mug onto the plate.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:07.12,0:46:09.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can see here there's not much changes in orientation.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:09.42,0:46:14.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you actually want to consider the orientation of then the vector as well we need to use remmanion flow matching.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:14.78,0:46:18.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in that case you have your data that are encoded on the sphere.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:18.30,0:46:22.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,So your actions are belonging to the surface of the sphere and we start from a base distribution which
Dialogue: 0,0:46:22.74,0:46:28.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,is in that case a Gaussian that you can really imagine as a Gaussian wrapped around the sphere essentially and
Dialogue: 0,0:46:28.19,0:46:33.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,the flow goes along a geodeic which is the shortest path possible on the manifold and this flow is
Dialogue: 0,0:46:33.89,0:46:39.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,defined on the tangent space at every single point by a vector field and then the principle is very similar.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:39.53,0:46:42.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,You define a desired vector field on every single tangent space.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:42.47,0:46:45.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,You match a neural network that you can condition an observation.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:45.82,0:46:50.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then uh to integrate you actually need to use the exponential map that
Dialogue: 0,0:46:50.05,0:46:53.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,will map the vector field on the tangent space onto the manifold of interest.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:53.79,0:46:57.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,And when you do that you can actually handle big changes in orientation.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:57.47,0:47:04.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is a remmanion flow matching policy trained again to rotate the mug and put it on the plate.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:04.64,0:47:10.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the flow matching policies that we just discussed handle geometric action spaces but they actually do not ensure safe behaviors.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:10.34,0:47:14.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can have these kind of issues uh where the the skill doesn't converge.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:14.83,0:47:22.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So a common way to um ensure stability is to model robot motion as dynamical systems that you make stable by construction and here we
Dialogue: 0,0:47:22.48,0:47:25.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,propose to model such dynamical systems via flow matching and to use
Dialogue: 0,0:47:25.79,0:47:31.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,inductive bias coming from control theory to ensure stable convergence to an equilibrium.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:31.68,0:47:34.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the main idea again in this case we are working on Lie groups.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:35.03,0:47:39.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,You define an desired equilibrium on the manifolds and you define a leapono function by hand
Dialogue: 0,0:47:39.84,0:47:45.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,that essentially will define a set of admissible velocity at every single point in your manifold.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:45.23,0:47:50.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what we propose to do is to learn a constraint flow matching model that will give you some of those velocities and
Dialogue: 0,0:47:50.99,0:48:00.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,essentially when you then obtain uh the flow from this vector field you will um converge with guarantees to the stable point on the manifold.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:00.24,0:48:02.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um the issue here is that if we have a simple yapon of function that
Dialogue: 0,0:48:02.98,0:48:08.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,allows us to have this admissible velocity set um they may only encode limited dynamic behaviors.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:08.28,0:48:13.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we want to increase expressivity and we can do so by learning an additional deformism that
Dialogue: 0,0:48:13.12,0:48:20.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,will move us from this simple dynamical system to something more complex that can match uh complex demonstrations.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:20.88,0:48:24.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what is really cool is that those stable flow matching dynamical system they are then
Dialogue: 0,0:48:24.37,0:48:30.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,able to combine the stability and expressivity of flow matching with formal stability guarantees from control theory.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:30.38,0:48:35.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,So essentially when you have new demonstration you can simply learn the flow matching vector field and then
Dialogue: 0,0:48:35.72,0:48:41.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,um whatever wherever you start in the space you will follow your demonstration and converge to a stable equilibrium.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:41.22,0:48:45.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we train such policies on the Lie group SE(3) to encode position and orientation.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:45.77,0:48:53.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,You see here two different types of examples and you can see that regardless of where we start we actually converge always to the equilibrium point.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:53.54,0:48:57.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,So to the goal here uh and those policies are also stable to preservation.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:57.42,0:49:02.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,We show that in the paper that you can see below.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:02.64,0:49:06.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in general we would like to even more enhance the generalizability of such systems and
Dialogue: 0,0:49:06.75,0:49:12.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,here we approach that through the lens of symmetries using uh on top of geometry group theory.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:12.34,0:49:15.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the main idea of symmetries for robot learning is to use either
Dialogue: 0,0:49:16.02,0:49:20.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,data augmentation or equivalent neural network to generalize the behavior of a policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:20.52,0:49:25.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,So for example, if your robot learns how to pick a mug with a given orientation of the handle,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:25.58,0:49:30.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,if you use such a network, you will be able to pick the mug with any types of orientation.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:30.32,0:49:32.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,So there are many other symmetries in robotics.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:32.16,0:49:36.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can think about for example scaling the mug where you want to learn to pick mugs that are smaller or bigger.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:36.72,0:49:44.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Or you may also want to have a policy trained on one of the arms of the robot and be able to replicate it immediately with the other arm.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:44.64,0:49:50.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um so such policies are well developed in the literature but they typically handle only one single symmetry at a time.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:50.64,0:49:56.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Although many many tasks actually will benefit from taking into account several of those symmetries at the same time.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:56.70,0:50:00.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what we wanted to see here is if we can compose all those different
Dialogue: 0,0:50:00.48,0:50:05.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,symmetries and in general this is difficult because the symmetry arise in different types of spaces.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:05.14,0:50:11.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if you think here about the planer rotation and the scaling both of them arise in the task space they are concerned about the object and um if
Dialogue: 0,0:50:11.59,0:50:15.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,you think then about the the morphological symmetry here that generalizes to the left
Dialogue: 0,0:50:15.66,0:50:20.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,and the right arm this arise in the configuration space of your system at hand.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:20.64,0:50:26.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what we propose to do is to view symmetries through the lens of vector field where we essentially define uh a vector
Dialogue: 0,0:50:26.90,0:50:32.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,field that tells you how here in this case the mug is rotating according to the symmetry and then what we can do is
Dialogue: 0,0:50:32.14,0:50:40.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,to use the geometry of the forward kinematic map to lift this vector field horizontally onto the configuration space and this essentially tells you
Dialogue: 0,0:50:40.49,0:50:47.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,how the configuration of the robot will change when you want to have the mock um to handle the mug rotated in different ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:47.31,0:50:50.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can do the same uh in this case for the scaling.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:50.49,0:50:57.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,And once we have all um the vector field in the same space, we can simply combine them by composing the different vector fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:57.26,0:51:02.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with that, you can actually handle uh symmetries in a composition.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:02.40,0:51:07.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we have now here an example where my PhD student is demonstrating the robot how to um draw a
