Dialogue: 0,0:51:26.37,0:51:32.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,And here the only demonstration that recorded are the one that you see on the top right.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:32.48,0:51:40.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here composing symmetries leads to enhanced generalizability of the skills and also more data efficiency.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:40.56,0:51:45.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here I showed you example of how geometry allows us to learn robot skills involving full poses and how when we
Dialogue: 0,0:51:45.76,0:51:53.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,combine it with inductive biases from control and from group theory it also leads to stable generalizable and data efficient robot policies.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:53.84,0:51:56.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,So for the second part of the talk I would like to turn our attentions
Dialogue: 0,0:51:56.88,0:52:01.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,to the objects that the robot has to manipulate and specifically focusing on deformable objects.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:01.91,0:52:07.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,So when we want to manipulate such objects essentially what we typically do is that we build a model.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:07.92,0:52:12.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here the idea is that given the position the velocity of your object and the force you
Dialogue: 0,0:52:12.48,0:52:16.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,are applying onto it you would like to know what is the acceleration that the object is going
Dialogue: 0,0:52:16.05,0:52:20.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,to take and once you have such a model simply what we can do is to infer which
Dialogue: 0,0:52:20.08,0:52:25.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,is the force that we need to apply on the object such that it follows a desired trajectory.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:25.36,0:52:30.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is really difficult with deformable objects is that they have high or even infinite dimensional configuration spaces
Dialogue: 0,0:52:30.96,0:52:37.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,which mean they have basically infinite degrees of freedom and they also have nonlinear and very complex dynamics.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:37.76,0:52:44.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what we propose to do here is to take inductive bias from geometry but also from physics and to account for the fact that those
Dialogue: 0,0:52:44.73,0:52:49.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,objects follow langian dynamics which can be represented on a high dimensional remmanion
Dialogue: 0,0:52:49.05,0:52:53.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,manifold that is completely described by the mass inertia matrix of your system.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:53.76,0:52:56.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,So essentially what we'd like to do is to have we have this
Dialogue: 0,0:52:56.71,0:53:00.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,unknown highdimensional langent dynamics and we would like to learn the parameters of that.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:00.57,0:53:06.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what we propose to do here is to take the manifold hypothesis and to approximate trajectories on this highdimensional
Dialogue: 0,0:53:07.04,0:53:14.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,manifold on which the langian dynamics are evolving by trajectories on a much lower dimensional manifold that keeps the same structure.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:14.88,0:53:23.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,So essentially we want to approximate our high dimensional Lagrangian dynamics by much lower dimensional Lagrangian dynamics again.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:23.28,0:53:25.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we need two key elements to do that.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:25.68,0:53:28.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first one is an encoder decoder framework.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:28.53,0:53:36.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we use a geometric biorthogonal autoencoder where essentially the geometry that you have in this autoencoder allows you to
Dialogue: 0,0:53:36.08,0:53:42.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,maintain the langion structure um that we know arise in the high dimensional space but into the latent space in that case.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:42.19,0:53:46.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and here the parameters of this autoenccoder are um points on the
Dialogue: 0,0:53:46.56,0:53:51.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Riemannian manifolds and then we train all of these using actually Riemannian optimization.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:51.36,0:53:55.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,The second key element is what allows you to learn the dynamics in the latent space.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:55.29,0:53:57.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,It is a geometric Lagrangian neural network.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:57.20,0:54:01.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,So again inductive biases from geometry and from physics and here we account for the
Dialogue: 0,0:54:01.23,0:54:05.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,fact that the mass inertia matrix belong to the manifold of symmetric positive definite matrices.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:05.70,0:54:08.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,So again we train this via remmanion optimization.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:08.13,0:54:13.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what is really cool here is that the physical laws of our highdimensional deformable
Dialogue: 0,0:54:13.82,0:54:19.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,objects are completely preserved here both by the geometric mappings and the latent geometric Lagrangian neural network.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:19.12,0:54:24.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you are guaranteed that whatever your model is actually predicting follows the laws of physics.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:25.04,0:54:31.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we have an example and this is simply comparing the geometric Lagrangian neural network with respect to a classical neural network for a
Dialogue: 0,0:54:31.63,0:54:34.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,two degrees of freedom pendalum system and you see that after a
Dialogue: 0,0:54:34.78,0:54:39.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,few seconds the classical neural network starts diverging while the geometric one doesn't.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:39.50,0:54:42.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and we also learn here with much more data efficiency.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:42.94,0:54:46.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can check again the referred paper for that.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:46.24,0:54:47.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can then move to high dimensional systems.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:47.98,0:54:53.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we have this 600 degrees of freedom cloth that we approximate with a 10-dimensional latent space.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:53.10,0:54:59.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you see that our model is actually very nicely able to model the dynamics of the cloth.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:59.36,0:55:04.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,And here again this is really thanks to the geometry that we have both in the mappings and in the Lagrangian neural network.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:04.73,0:55:10.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,If we don't have the geometry I cannot even show you here what this will actually give.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:10.40,0:55:14.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is also really nice with those models is that we can then make use of the learn latent space for control.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:14.96,0:55:19.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,First by defining a desired trajectory um that we know in the high dimensional space
Dialogue: 0,0:55:19.22,0:55:22.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,onto the latent space and then we can apply in this case a simple PD
Dialogue: 0,0:55:22.80,0:55:27.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,plus controller in the latent space which is composed of a forward fit forward term
Dialogue: 0,0:55:27.68,0:55:33.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,um made of the learned latent dynamic and of a latent um proportional derivative term.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:33.21,0:55:36.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then once we have the desired control comment in latent space,
Dialogue: 0,0:55:36.67,0:55:41.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,we can map it back onto the high dimension space based on the mappings that we learned.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:41.35,0:55:45.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,The big advantage of this approach is that we can derive control guarantees on it.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:45.77,0:55:49.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,So essentially here this method is locally exponential input to state stabil
Dialogue: 0,0:55:49.77,0:55:56.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,stable which means that your error is decreasing exponentially and is upper bounded.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:56.56,0:56:01.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can see here in example we learned a model of a pendulum mesh system of 15 degrees of freedom.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:01.44,0:56:02.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,We reduced it to three.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:02.73,0:56:06.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And here we are aiming at tracking the black circle that you see uh in the background.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:06.96,0:56:15.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you see that again our controller is much better able to track the circle compared to a model that doesn't have any idea about uh the dynamics.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:15.20,0:56:17.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can go also to more complex real world system.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:17.71,0:56:22.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we had an underactuated plush monkey puppet manipulated by the robot.
